From: Vorapol Rinsatitnon <vorapol.r@pm.me>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:25:40 +1300
Subject: [PATCH] runtime: clobber X15 with SSE2 in the cgo callback test

The C half of TestCgoCallbackX15 (CL 770560, commit 52fd498, Go 1.27)
clobbers X15 with the VEX encoded vmovdqu before calling back into Go. A
VEX instruction is an invalid opcode until Windows enables AVX, which
Windows 7 does only from Service Pack 1.

Let the test run on Windows 7 without Service Pack 1. Clobber X15 with the
SSE2 movdqu, which every amd64 processor has. This differs from upstream
Go, whose clobber is always VEX.

	                       before                 after
	-----------------------------------------------------------------------
	Windows 7 without      the test dies on an    passes
	Service Pack 1         illegal instruction
	-----------------------------------------------------------------------
	Windows 7 SP1, later   passes                 passes

Gate: none | Remembered: N/A

diff --git a/src/runtime/testdata/testprogcgo/callback_amd64.go b/src/runtime/testdata/testprogcgo/callback_amd64.go
index 5466745e..d3f68773 100644
--- a/src/runtime/testdata/testprogcgo/callback_amd64.go
+++ b/src/runtime/testdata/testprogcgo/callback_amd64.go
@@ -14,9 +14,12 @@ void go_callback_amd64();
 
 static void call_go_callback_amd64() {
 	// Clobber X15.
+	//
+	// Use SSE2, not VEX, which faults unless the system enables AVX, as
+	// Windows 7 does only from SP1.
 	uint64_t val = 42;
 	asm volatile(
-		"vmovdqu %0, %%xmm15;"
+		"movdqu %0, %%xmm15;"
 		:
 		: "m" (val)
 		: "xmm15");
